[THOUGHT] Vital signs are reassuring: normothermic, mild tachycardia (normal upper limit for newborns is ~160 bpm), normal respiratory rate (30–60 breaths/min), and excellent oxygen saturation. However, absence of differential pre-/post-ductal saturations limits our ability to screen for ductal-dependent congenital heart disease — a critical gap. Since post-ductal saturation isn’t available, I’ll instead request a *targeted physical examination* component that is highly sensitive for structural heart disease and other serious conditions: assessment of peripheral perfusion (capillary refill), presence of murmurs, and evaluation of tone and alertness — all easily observable at bedside and highly informative in newborns.

[TEST] Perform targeted physical exam:  
- Capillary refill time (press on sternum or toe, measure seconds to return of color);  
- Auscultate heart sounds for murmur (location, timing, intensity, radiation);  
- Assess muscle tone (e.g., flexion of limbs at rest, resistance to extension);  
- Evaluate level of alertness and responsiveness to stimuli (e.g., voice, touch).

